WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:12.480
This may look like something out of a

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.340
horror film but it's actually a glass worm

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.080
it's the larval form of a very common

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.780
lake insect called the phantom midge

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.750
and it's evolved kind of a horrific way to

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.580
catch a zooplankton

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.860
these centimeter or so long worms ambush their prey

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.820
with long articulated bristles that rapidly

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.280
unfurl into a catching basket

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.400
recently researchers discovered exactly how this

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.420
capture mechanism works

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.400
they were using  high-speed video to study the

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.920
interactions between glass worms and one

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.000
of its main prey items water fleas

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.440
also known as Daphnia

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.200
they found that many

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.520
bristles prevent the prey from escaping

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.520
including long upper lip hairs that fan out

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.040
sideways during the glass worm attack

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.180
other researchers had doubted that these

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.180
lip hairs could move because

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.140
they lack muscle but they might be

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.040
powered by the pressure of internal fluids

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.640
these videos—shot at up to 8,000 fps—

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.940
also allowed the researchers to time the attack

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.180
The bristles reached their target in just

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.500
14 milliseconds—one of the fastest known strikes

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.220
for comparison the mantis shrimp

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.220
takes just 4 to 8 milliseconds

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.610
the record-holders: trap-jaw
ant's and trap-jaw spiders attack in

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.320
less than one millisecond

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.080
after the glass worm has its prey safely ensnared

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.586
it needs around 40 more milliseconds to retract

00:01:44.586 --> 00:01:46.560
and then swallow the prey

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.260
but as you can see from this
real-time footage

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.020
that's still pretty fast

